      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We can use the Pythagorean theorem to solve this problem. The traveler's journey forms a right triangle, with the north-south leg being 18 miles (north) and the east-west leg being 11 miles (west) + 6 miles (east) = 17 miles. The distance from the starting point is the hypotenuse of this right triangle.\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem, we have:\n\nc² = a² + b²\n\nwhere c is the hypotenuse (distance from the starting point), a is the north-south leg, and b is the east-west leg.\n\nc² = 18² + 17²\nc² = 324 + 289\nc² = 613\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we find:\n\nc = √613 ≈ 24.76 miles\n\nTherefore, the traveler was approximately 24.76 miles from the starting point after the four legs of the journey.\n\nThe final answer is \\boxed{24.76} miles.",
